Stochastic propagation models in packet-level 5G simulators struggle to reproduce site-specific multipath effects, while deterministic model based on ray tracing is computationally expensive for realistic simulation rates.
    To bridge this gap, we introduce \texttt{sionnart}, an integrated simulation framework that embeds the Sionna RT ray tracing engine directly into the ns-3 process via pybind11, exposing deterministic channel information to standard ns-3 propagation interfaces without the overhead of inter-process communication.
    To make ray tracing practical for mobile user equipment (UE), our framework employs two novel mechanisms: a displacement-based coherence cache that reuses channel parameters within a geometry-dependent validity radius, and an adaptive virtual receiver generation technique that proactively pre-computes near-future UE positions during a single batched Sionna RT query.
    In our framework performance benchmarking involving 32 UEs, \texttt{sionnart} outperform the standard socket-coupled \texttt{ns3sionna} baseline by a factor of 43.8 under high mobility, all while keeping the GPU memory footprint below 500~MiB.
    As a practical application case study, we demonstrate the framework's utility in a massive MIMO 5G deployment with 20 UEs and various gNB array sizes across free-space, urban macro, and urban micro environments.
    This case study reveals that deterministic multipath significantly alters critical metrics like CQI, MCS selection, throughput, and gNB energy consumption compared to stochastic models, particularly in geometry-sensitive deployments.
    These results confirm that our integrated simulation tool successfully makes repeatable, geometry-aware system-level evaluation both computationally viable and highly practical.
